  "spiritual_reading": "The state called pure is assigned to the inmost arcana of the higher heavens. It is called pure because it is the whole quality of wisdom that has never been touched by evil, and has not entered the conflict with hell.\n\nBut the state called self-restrained belongs to the arcana of that wisdom which did enter into obscurity and was mingled with falsity. In that mingling it was profaned in external forms, yet afterward it was raised again into wisdom; for this reason it is called self-restrained.\n\nMoreover, those who are fully purified, when they stand against hell, do not admit evil into themselves, nor are they overcome by it; they remain untouched by spiritual death.\n\nOn the other hand, the state called self-restrained is this: wisdom that was mingled with falsity is purified, is gathered up out of all the lower and disordered affections, and is fixed again in the higher heavens. It is called self-restrained because it first admitted the delight of evil, but afterward was cleansed of that delight and was made holy.\n\nFurthermore, the state called pure is that wisdom which is purified by the Divine pattern of mediation. It is raised into more interior degrees and is confirmed in a formed likeness of the heavenly order.\n\nAgain, the state called self-restrained is the remaining portion of that wisdom which, after purification, still lingers in lower things. It is raised and it descends again through successive changes of state. Part of it also comes to be embodied in the outermost human life; and even that, when it is chosen in that outer life, receives faith in deliverance and becomes holy. Therefore it too is named self-restrained.\n\nMoreover, the state named pure is the Divine Understanding when it comes and takes on the formed qualities of those who are being saved. But the state called self-restrained is the new will and understanding in a person when it is purified from the former self. For it is cleansed, it casts off the evil conjoined with it, and it becomes self-restrained.\n\nEven in the outermost life, what is called pure is a person whose love has never been bound to the delights that profane it and has not been defiled by their conjunction. In contrast, what is called self-restrained is a person who has lived with such delights in the world, but afterward is cleansed from them and renounces them; and by this turning away he is made holy in self-restraint.\n\nSee, these are the kinds of the pure and the self-restrained in many aspects; for purity and self-restraint exist in many forms.",
